digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56912-9
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes to develop and evaluate a confined dry-chemical interlayer growth strategy in which molten precursors are infiltrated into the fixed interlayer cavities of the lamellar template RUB-15 to form ultrathin monolayer, high-crystallinity 2D metal oxide and 2D metal sulfide nanosheets, offering a versatile alternative to exfoliation and wet-chemical routes.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When existing top-down and wet-chemical bottom-up routes for 2D metal oxides are limited, and confinement in interlayer cavities can restrict nanosheet thickness, it is scientifically motivated to develop and evaluate a confined interlayer growth route for ultrathin 2D materials."];
  "N_Feasibility" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Longitudinal dimension can be strictly limited, and Guo's group reported monolayer carbon, so confinement synthesis is feasible for 2D materials."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Because two-dimensional solids are distinguished by constrained longitudinal dimensionality and 2D materials including oxides have interesting properties and promising applications, 2D metal oxides are a scientifically interesting class to investigate."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Because exfoliation-based top-down formation is limited for non-layered metal oxides and wet-chemical/liquid-phase bottom-up routes face precursor hydrolysis constraints, current 2D metal oxide fabrication has important route-dependent limitations."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) The scientific interest of 2D metal oxides together with route-dependent fabrication limitations motivates seeking a versatile and facile method for 2D metal oxide synthesis."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The need for a versatile and facile 2D metal oxide synthesis method, together with evidence that confinement synthesis can restrict product thickness in interlayer cavities, motivates investigating a confined interlayer growth strategy."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Examples of oxidized layered metal oxide formation and liquid-phase exfoliation of metal oxide sheets show that exfoliation can be used in some metal-oxide-sheet preparation routes."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) For exfoliation-based metal oxide sheet formation, the relevant successful examples are tied to layered or sheet-like oxide structures rather than to arbitrary non-layered metal oxides."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Since exfoliation-based metal oxide sheet formation is tied to layered or sheet-like oxide structures and most metal oxides are not intrinsically layered, exfoliation-based formation of 2D metal oxides is limited."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) CVD and wet-chemical synthesis are bottom-up routes for 2D metal oxide preparation, with CVD facing scale/type/yield restrictions and wet-chemical synthesis offering low-cost, high-yield, simple operation."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Zhao et al.'s graphene-oxide-template synthesis is an example of an ultrathin 2D metal oxide made by a wet-chemical bottom-up route."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Wet-chemical or liquid-phase bottom-up synthesis of ultrathin 2D metal oxides can be limited by precursor pH, solubility, and hydrolysis behavior, especially when precursors hydrolyze violently."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) General pattern: Two-dimensional solids are distinguished from bulk solids by constrained longitudinal dimensionality."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently there is the fact that most metal oxides are not intrinsically layered."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) Specific instance: CVD large-scale application is restricted by high energy intensity, limited product types and low yield."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) General pattern: The wet-chemical synthesis method benefits from low-cost, high-yield and simple operation."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) Specific instance: Zhao et al. synthesized ultrathin 2D metal oxides using graphene oxide as template."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) Specific instance: The synthesis process is restricted by the solution pH, solubility and hydrolysis of precursors."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) General pattern: A strategy using surfaces of water-soluble salt crystals as growth substrates has been discovered."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Process is restricted by pH/hydrolysis, and salt crystal strategy exists, so it remains challenging if precursors hydrolyze violently."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Specific instance: 2D materials including graphene and oxides have interesting properties and promising applications."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) Specific instance: Confinement synthesis is the transverse growth of 2D target guest species by utilizing layer spacing."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Confinement synthesis utilizes layer spacing, and the method is flexible, so longitudinal dimension of target products can be strictly limited."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Observed phenomenon is Guo's group reported solution preparation of monolayer carbon within interlayer cavity."];
  "S31" [label="(31,0,0) General pattern: The method is flexible for the construction of other ultrathin 2D frameworks."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) General pattern: Mechanical, chemical, ultrasonic exfoliation, CVD, template, and hydrothermal methods have been used to fabricate 2D architectures."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Various methods exist, and exfoliation produced oxides, so top-down strategy is similar to graphene fabrication."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Specific instance: Nine types of 2D hexagonal metal oxide monolayers have been prepared via exfoliation method."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Specific instance: Bulk metal surface could be polished and oxidized to layered metal oxide in oxygen-deficient environment."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) General pattern: Liquid-phase exfoliation was used in metal oxide sheets production."];
  "S4" -> "S5" [label="induction-common"];
